  Rewrite by m0us3r - https://github.com/mym0us3r/Unified-Sysmon-Configs
  Date: 07/24/2026 Friday

  Sysmon File Executable Detected rules: 100800 - 100810
  Rewritten for Sysmon Native (Windows 11 24H2+)
  Provider: Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon
  EID 29: FileExecutableDetected - new PE file drop detection
  MITRE ATT&CK v15 aligned

  ESCAPING REFERENCE (critical):
    Wazuh decoder doubles backslashes in Windows paths.
    Stored internally: C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe (double backslash)
    PCRE2 to match \\: use \\\\
    Rule: path separators -> \\\\ | regex meta (\s \d \b \.) -> \\

  CONTEXT:
    EID 29 fires when a new PE (executable) file is written to disk.
    sysmon-native.xml already scopes this to Downloads and
    C:\Users\Public\ with an IMPHASH condition (T1204-New-Executable
    Rule). This event already fired during this project's own live
    testing (note_masq3.exe, 2026-07-23) via rule T1204-New-Executable -
    the telemetry and Sysmon-side filtering are proven to work; this
    file adds the missing Wazuh-side routing and alerting layer that
    was absent until now.

  STRUCTURAL FIX (2026-07-24): anchor 100800 was originally level=10,
  breaking from this project's dominant convention. Lowered to level=0
  for consistency, even though this EID is already fairly narrow
  (Downloads/Public + IMPHASH) - the more specific child rule (100801,
  dropped by a scripting interpreter) carries the real signal.

  MIGRATION NOTES:
    ANCHOR: if_group>sysmon_event_29, assigned by rule 100850 in
    0595-win-sysmon_rules.xml (uses the officially reserved custom-rule
    range, not the 61xxx dispatch sequence - see 0595's header for why).
    This is a NEW file - not yet field-tested as a Wazuh rule
    (the underlying Sysmon telemetry has been field-tested). Validate
    with wazuh-analysisd -t and a live test before treating as
    production-confirmed.
-->

<group name="sysmon,sysmon_eid29,windows,">

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BASE ANCHOR - EID 29 (File Executable Detected)
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <rule id="100800" level="0">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_29</if_group>
    <field name="win.system.providerName">^Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon$</field>
    <description>Native Sysmon [EID 29]: New executable file detected - $(win.eventdata.targetFilename)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204</id>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
  </rule>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       T1204 / T1105 - New executable created by a scripting interpreter
       or downloader (higher-confidence than the anchor alone: the
       *process that dropped it* is itself suspicious).
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <rule id="100801" level="13">
    <if_sid>100800</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\\\(powershell|cmd|wscript|cscript|mshta|certutil|curl|wget)\.exe$</field>
    <description>CRITICAL: New executable dropped by $(win.eventdata.image) - $(win.eventdata.targetFilename)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204</id>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
  </rule>

</group>
